{
 "rule": "Phase 2e PC-only analysis rule, written 2026-11-01T15:46:27+07:00 before the analysis script ai/automation/analyze_q2e_pc.py was run. Not from a source. Not reviewed by the user (user request of 2026-11-01 to finish the remaining unknowns before phase 3). Reviewed by the advisor (inventory results/phase2e_unknowns_inventory.md and its traps). No threshold is used, every statement is descriptive and carries no mechanism.\n\nG7a (page 01 section 4.5, why Resize with asymmetric and nearest_mode floor is placed on the NPU). Input: the ONNX the toolkit consumed for the baseline build (bench/onboard_q2c/diag/ppe5_yolov8n_baseline_100ep_s0/int8_input.onnx, two Resize nodes, mode nearest, coordinate_transformation_mode asymmetric, nearest_mode floor). Method: onnxruntime on a single-node Resize micro model (input 1x256x20x20 random normal seed 0, scales 1,1,2,2) for the attribute sets (asymmetric,floor) as reference, (half_pixel,round_prefer_floor), (half_pixel,floor), (pytorch_half_pixel,round_prefer_floor), (align_corners,round_prefer_floor) from the Operator List values (OPL p87 lists half_pixel, pytorch_half_pixel, align_corners and says nearest_mode cannot be set). Report max abs difference to the reference for scale 2, and for scales 1.5 and 3 (output sizes 30 and 60) where a difference is possible. Reading, fixed now: a zero difference at scale 2 for a substitute set only says the two attribute sets select the same source index for the YOLO shape. It does not say what the compiler does with the attribute, and it does not explain why the node lands on the NPU. A non-zero difference at scale 1.5 or 3 only says a board micro model at that scale can discriminate whether the NPU honours the attribute (a separate board experiment, not in this rule).\n\nG10 (page 01 section 5.2, 8 Reshape ONNX nodes against 7 Reshape rows, Split). Input: the same ONNX (237 nodes) and the first Network Layer Information Table of the verbose build log bench/onboard_q2c/logs/build_ppe5_yolov8n_baseline_100ep_s0_base.log. Method: count nodes per op type in the ONNX and rows per OpType in the table, then for Reshape list each ONNX Reshape node name and whether a row FullName contains it, and for Split and Slice the same counts. Reading: descriptive only, a row count difference is reported with the names that have no row. No claim about which pass removed it.\n\nM1 (page 02 section 2.2 and 6.1, split rule of librknnrt). Input: layers JSON of the g4b CORE_0_1_2 sessions (bench/onboard_g4b/tier1_int8_CORE_0_1_2_sess*_layers.json, SiLU and ReLU models). Method: for every row with at least two cores above 0 percent, list op, name, output shape (from the build table), the three workload percentages, and per op type the number of rows split and not split. Reading: an observed pattern of this model and this runtime version, never a rule. The rule itself lives in the binary and stays unknown.\n\nQAT FP16 rows (page 15 section 3, the 8 FLOAT16 NPU rows). Input: first table of results of the QAT verbose build log (bench/onboard_q2c/logs/qat_train.log) and of the split PTQ build log. Method: print the rows from the first non-INT8 NPU row minus 3 to the last plus 3 of the QAT table with id, op, dtype and FullName, and the rows of the split PTQ table with the same OpType Resize and their neighbours. Reading: descriptive, which neighbours the FP16 rows have. No claim about why.\n\nLimits: PC only, one model family, one toolkit version 2.3.2. Budget: one analysis script, run once, unchanged after the data is seen except to fix a crash.\n\nADDENDUM 2026-11-01T15:47:48+07:00: after the first run two defects of the method were found in the script, not in the data: (1) the G10 check \"a row FullName contains the node name\" matches a prefix of another name (for example /model/model.22/Reshape inside Reshape_1), so the exact node name after the \"Op:\" prefix is compared instead and both results are kept in the JSON; (2) the M1 output shape was looked up by the layer id of the board table, ids differ between the board table and the build table (page 01 says to select by op name), so the shape is now looked up by FullName. No statement of the rule is changed.\n",
